1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,195
Retrieval quality gets judged the same way most things get judged before someone

2
00:00:05,195 --> 00:00:10,100
learns better: a few queries, typed in by whoever built the feature, all of which

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:11,009
happen to work.

4
00:00:11,009 --> 00:00:12,220
That's not evidence.

5
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:17,186
Section four already taught you why: a small, self-selected sample flatters you by

6
00:00:17,186 --> 00:00:17,973
construction.

7
00:00:17,973 --> 00:00:22,576
This lecture gives you three specific, plain-language questions to ask about

8
00:00:22,576 --> 00:00:26,392
retrieval instead, and the scorecard to actually ask them with.

9
00:00:26,392 --> 00:00:31,237
Notice, too, why measuring retrieval separately from the final answer matters at

10
00:00:31,237 --> 00:00:31,479
all.

11
00:00:31,479 --> 00:00:36,566
If you only ever check whether the finished answer looked right, a retrieval problem

12
00:00:36,566 --> 00:00:41,048
and a generation problem produce the exact same symptom: a wrong response.

13
00:00:41,048 --> 00:00:46,135
You'd know something failed and have no way to know which of the two stages actually

14
00:00:46,135 --> 00:00:46,559
did it.

15
00:00:46,559 --> 00:00:51,464
Scoring retrieval on its own is what turns "the bot got it wrong" into "the right

16
00:00:51,464 --> 00:00:56,188
chunk was never found" or "it was found and misread," two completely different

17
00:00:56,188 --> 00:00:56,551
fixes.

18
00:00:57,101 --> 00:01:01,669
Three questions, and notice they catch three completely different failures.

19
00:01:01,669 --> 00:01:06,541
Hit rate is the blunt one: for a question with a known right answer somewhere in

20
00:01:06,541 --> 00:01:11,596
your corpus, did retrieval surface it anywhere in the results, yes or no. Rank goes

21
00:01:11,596 --> 00:01:16,650
a step further, because finding the right chunk at position nine when you only keep

22
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:19,695
the top five is the same as not finding it at all.

23
00:01:19,695 --> 00:01:24,689
And precision looks at the other direction entirely: of what you did retrieve, how

24
00:01:24,689 --> 00:01:29,805
much was actually relevant, versus padding that dilutes the context the model has to

25
00:01:29,805 --> 00:01:30,414
work with.

26
00:01:30,414 --> 00:01:35,469
You'll hear these called recall at k, mean reciprocal rank, and precision at k in a

27
00:01:35,469 --> 00:01:37,296
real engineering conversation.

28
00:01:37,296 --> 00:01:39,184
Same three ideas, formal names.

29
00:01:39,184 --> 00:01:43,690
Use them, and you'll sound like you've done this before, because you have.

30
00:01:43,690 --> 00:01:48,380
Notice the shape of this table matches the p95 lesson from Section two almost

31
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:48,867
exactly.

32
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:53,921
Hit rate alone is an average dressed up as a metric: it tells you retrieval usually

33
00:01:53,921 --> 00:01:54,287
works.

34
00:01:54,287 --> 00:01:56,601
But what does "usually" actually mean?

35
00:01:56,601 --> 00:02:01,351
Rank and precision are the tail: the specific cases where it technically found

36
00:02:01,351 --> 00:02:04,823
something and that something wasn't good enough to trust.

37
00:02:05,373 --> 00:02:10,111
Here's what an honest first scorecard tends to look like, and it's usually less

38
00:02:10,111 --> 00:02:11,851
flattering than the demo was.

39
00:02:11,851 --> 00:02:16,709
Eight of ten found the right source somewhere, which sounds fine until you notice

40
00:02:16,709 --> 00:02:21,627
the average rank: third out of five kept, meaning it's riding close to getting cut

41
00:02:21,627 --> 00:02:23,487
if anyone tightens that number.

42
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:28,225
And only sixty percent of what actually got retrieved was relevant, which means

43
00:02:28,225 --> 00:02:33,144
nearly half the context handed to the model on a typical query was noise it had to

44
00:02:33,144 --> 00:02:33,743
read past.

45
00:02:33,743 --> 00:02:38,482
None of these numbers show up in a five query demo, because a demo doesn't keep

46
00:02:38,482 --> 00:02:38,842
score.

47
00:02:38,842 --> 00:02:43,460
Read those two missing queries carefully too, not just the eight that worked.

48
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:48,139
The two the system never found at all are the most important row on this whole

49
00:02:48,139 --> 00:02:52,997
scorecard, because a rank problem is something the existing pipeline can improve.

50
00:02:52,997 --> 00:02:57,916
A pure miss usually points somewhere upstream entirely, back to chunking from last

51
00:02:57,916 --> 00:03:02,534
lecture, or ingestion never having included that document in the first place.

52
00:03:03,084 --> 00:03:07,448
Say the honest caveat, because a clean retrieval scorecard can create false

53
00:03:07,448 --> 00:03:09,776
confidence about the feature as a whole.

54
00:03:09,776 --> 00:03:14,432
Retrieval and generation are two separate stages with two separate ways to fail.

55
00:03:14,432 --> 00:03:19,029
You can retrieve exactly the right chunk and still watch the model summarise it

56
00:03:19,029 --> 00:03:22,055
wrong, miss a caveat inside it, or misread a number.

57
00:03:22,055 --> 00:03:24,848
This scorecard only measures the retrieval half.

58
00:03:24,848 --> 00:03:28,747
It's a necessary instrument, and on its own it's an incomplete one.

59
00:03:28,747 --> 00:03:33,460
Pair it with the rubric from Section four, which measures the half this scorecard

60
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:34,042
can't see.

61
00:03:34,042 --> 00:03:38,232
Think of the two together as a diagnostic order, not a duplicate effort.

62
00:03:38,232 --> 00:03:42,771
Run this scorecard first, because if retrieval itself is failing, no amount of

63
00:03:42,771 --> 00:03:47,019
rubric scoring on the final answer tells you where to actually intervene.

64
00:03:47,019 --> 00:03:51,849
Once retrieval is solid, the rubric from Section four is what tells you whether the

65
00:03:51,849 --> 00:03:55,515
model is doing its job with the material it was actually given.

66
00:03:56,065 --> 00:04:00,139
That's the artifact for today, and notice it's deliberately small.

67
00:04:00,139 --> 00:04:05,200
Ten questions, scored by hand, tells you more than an elaborate dashboard built on

68
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:06,558
data nobody's checked.

69
00:04:06,558 --> 00:04:08,533
Do it the unglamorous way first.

70
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:13,656
Pick ten real questions, know the right source for each one yourself before you run

71
00:04:13,656 --> 00:04:16,434
anything, and score what actually comes back.

72
00:04:16,434 --> 00:04:20,754
The manual pass is what makes the automated version trustworthy later.

73
00:04:20,754 --> 00:04:23,964
Resist the urge to skip straight to automating this.

74
00:04:23,964 --> 00:04:28,964
Ten scored by hand, once, teaches you what "relevant" actually means for your own

75
00:04:28,964 --> 00:04:34,086
content, the exact judgment call that would otherwise get made invisibly by whoever

76
00:04:34,086 --> 00:04:36,123
writes the automated check later.

77
00:04:36,673 --> 00:04:38,374
One thing before you move on.

78
00:04:38,374 --> 00:04:43,124
The scorecard template is A07, attached here, with all three metrics built in and

79
00:04:43,124 --> 00:04:44,942
space for ten queries to start.

80
00:04:44,942 --> 00:04:49,633
Run it against ten real questions from your own knowledge base, scored honestly,

81
00:04:49,633 --> 00:04:53,445
even when the numbers are worse than the demo led you to believe.

82
00:04:53,445 --> 00:04:58,313
Next lecture takes these numbers somewhere uncomfortable: the six specific places a

83
00:04:58,313 --> 00:05:02,828
RAG system that scored well in testing still breaks once it meets production.
